---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Panel from '../components/Panel.astro';
import { withBase } from '../lib/url';
import { readLastRun, freshness } from '../lib/state';
import { sourceLabel } from '../lib/record';
import {
  accessStatuses,
  authorityOf,
  resourceKinds,
  terms,
  typesOfKind,
} from '../lib/vocabulary';

const lastRun = readLastRun();
const fresh = freshness(lastRun);
const sources = Object.entries(lastRun?.sources ?? {});

// The Definitions section renders `vocabulary.yaml` directly (ADR-0040). It is
// the same file the tooltip on every chip reads, so the page explaining a term
// and the chip using it cannot say different things — which is exactly how they
// drifted before.
const kinds = resourceKinds.map((kind) => ({ kind, types: typesOfKind(kind.name) }));
---

<Base
  title="About — IEA Wind Data Catalogue"
  description="How the IEA Wind Data Catalogue is built: aggregation only, provenance on every field, and no registration."
>
  <div class="prose stack-6">
    <h1>How this works</h1>

    <p>
      Nobody who has already described a dataset on Zenodo is going to log into a
      second system and describe it again. So this catalogue does not ask them
      to. It watches the places IEA Wind people already publish and reflects what
      it finds.
    </p>

    <h2>What it does</h2>
    <p>
      A scheduled job reads seven sources — Zenodo, DataCite, Crossref, GitHub,
      OSTI, the Wind Data Hub and iea-wind.org — and writes what it finds into an
      append-only event log. The records you see are derived from that log and
      regenerated from it; delete them and they come back byte for byte.
    </p>

    <h2>What it will not do</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>It never edits a source.</strong> If a Zenodo record has a typo'd
        title, then a typo'd title is what Zenodo says, and the fix belongs at
        Zenodo — where the author can make it and every other consumer benefits.
        Every record carries a link to report an issue at its source.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>It never accepts an identifier from a model.</strong> Where a
        page has to be read by a language model, identifiers are extracted by
        pattern and resolved against DataCite or Crossref before the record
        exists. A DOI that does not resolve is dropped and logged.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>It never mirrors a file.</strong> Metadata and links only. The
        availability badge on each record says plainly whether the files are open,
        restricted, embargoed or behind a registration wall.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>It never deletes a record.</strong> When something vanishes
        upstream the page stays, with a notice, so existing citations keep
        resolving. Link rot is the failure mode a catalogue exists to fight.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="inclusion">What belongs in this catalogue</h2>
    <blockquote class="rule">
      <p>
        Things that belong in the catalogue must either be directly attributed
        to IEA Wind (ie be tagged, or published in our communities or websites
        in some way), or cited by work which is in the catalogue, or cite things
        which are in the catalogue. And ideally we should record which of those
        is the case.
      </p>
    </blockquote>

    <p>
      So every record says which. The three tests, in the order they are
      applied:
    </p>
    <dl class="defs__list">
      <dt>Directly attributed to IEA Wind</dt>
      <dd>
        It was found through an IEA Wind Zenodo community, an IEA Wind GitHub
        organisation, an iea-wind.org Task page, or a search of DataCite,
        Crossref or OSTI that names IEA Wind — or it carries an IEA Wind Task
        attribution. The route a record arrived by is recorded when it is
        harvested, because by the time you are holding the metadata there is
        often nothing left in it to say why it is here.
      </dd>
      <dt>Cites something in the catalogue</dt>
      <dd>
        Taken from published reference lists. A paper using the IEA 15 MW
        reference wind turbine belongs in a catalogue of IEA Wind output whether
        or not anybody tagged it.
      </dd>
      <dt>Cited by something in the catalogue</dt>
      <dd>The same edge, read from the other end.</dd>
    </dl>

    <p>
      A record meeting none of the three is <strong>not listed</strong> — but it
      is not deleted either. Its page and its URL stay, so anything already
      pointing at it keeps working, and the test is re-applied every time the
      catalogue is rebuilt: something that later gets cited by a Task report
      appears in the listings on its own.
    </p>
    <p>
      This matters because the catalogue casts a wide net on purpose. One of the
      ways it looks for software is a GitHub topic search, and
      <code>wind-energy</code> is a label anyone can put on anything — which is
      how a company API directory, a wind-farm video game and a
      nuclear-versus-wind comparison arrived here. They are all still at their
      URLs. None of them is in the catalogue.
    </p>

    <h2 id="definitions">Definitions</h2>
    <p>
      Three questions came back from IEA Wind about what the words on a record
      actually mean. They were fair questions, so every term the catalogue shows
      is defined here, and the same definition appears in the tooltip on the chip
      that uses it. Where a term is not ours to define — IEA Wind's own
      publication types, the FAIR4RS definition of research software — the
      definition is quoted and the authority named.
    </p>

    <h3 id="definitions-kind">Kind, and the specific type</h3>
    <p>
      Every record has a <strong>kind</strong> — the wide bucket you filter by
      first — and a more specific <strong>type</strong> under it. The type is
      derived from what the source already published: Zenodo's resource type and
      subtype, OSTI's product type, Crossref's type, DataCite's resource type,
      and the IEA Wind publication types, which appear only in a document's
      title. Both are filterable, so you can ask for every report, or only the
      Recommended Practices.
    </p>

    {kinds.map(({ kind, types }) => (
      <section class="defs" aria-labelledby={`def-kind-${kind.name}`}>
        <h4 id={`def-kind-${kind.name}`}>{kind.label}</h4>
        <p>{kind.definition}</p>
        {types.length > 0 && (
          <dl class="defs__list">
            {types.map((type) => (
              <>
                <dt>{type.label}</dt>
                <dd>
                  {type.definition}
                  {authorityOf(type) && (
                    <>
                      {' '}Defined by{' '}
                      {authorityOf(type)?.url ? (
                        <a href={authorityOf(type)?.url} rel="noopener external">
                          {authorityOf(type)?.title}
                        </a>
                      ) : (
                        authorityOf(type)?.title
                      )}.
                    </>
                  )}
                </dd>
              </>
            ))}
          </dl>
        )}
      </section>
    ))}

    <h3 id="definitions-availability">Availability</h3>
    <p>
      What stands between you and the files. The catalogue never implies a
      download that needs an account, and never assumes access the source has not
      stated.
    </p>
    <dl class="defs__list">
      {accessStatuses.map((status) => (
        <>
          <dt>{status.label}</dt>
          <dd>{status.definition}</dd>
        </>
      ))}
    </dl>

    <h3 id="definitions-terms">Everything else on a record</h3>
    <dl class="defs__list">
      {terms.map((term) => (
        <>
          <dt>{term.label}</dt>
          <dd>{term.definition}</dd>
        </>
      ))}
    </dl>

    <h2>Provenance</h2>
    <p>
      Every field records how it was known: read from an API, matched by pattern,
      or inferred by a model. Machine-inferred fields carry a violet badge with
      the model and its confidence, everywhere on the site. Being honest about
      which metadata is inferred is the difference between a catalogue people
      trust and one that gets dismissed the first time somebody spots a
      confidently wrong abstract.
    </p>

    <h2>Corrections</h2>
    <p>
      Corrections are additive and local: a curator can attach a note, add a Task
      attribution, or link a related record, but the upstream value keeps being
      displayed verbatim beside the note. Both truths on the page.
    </p>

    <h2>Machine access</h2>
    <p>
      Every record page carries schema.org <code>Dataset</code> JSON-LD, so the
      catalogue is indexable by Google Dataset Search. The whole catalogue is
      also published as <a href={withBase('/catalog.jsonld')}>DCAT</a>, and the
      underlying records are CKAN <code>package</code> dicts — the format a CKAN
      instance would ingest unmodified, should there ever be a reason to run one.
    </p>

    <h2 id="status">Status — the last run</h2>
    {fresh.neverRun ? (
      <Panel tone="info" icon="ℹ">
        <p>No run report in this checkout yet.</p>
      </Panel>
    ) : (
      <>
        <p>
          Harvest {lastRun?.harvest_version} finished {fresh.finishedAt}, wrote{' '}
          {lastRun?.records?.written ?? 0} record
          {lastRun?.records?.written === 1 ? '' : 's'} and appended{' '}
          {lastRun?.events_appended ?? 0} event
          {lastRun?.events_appended === 1 ? '' : 's'}.
        </p>
        {sources.length > 0 && (
          <table class="sources scroll-x">
            <caption class="visually-hidden">Per-source result of the last harvest</caption>
            <thead>
              <tr><th scope="col">Source</th><th scope="col">Seen</th><th scope="col">Changed</th><th scope="col">State</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              {sources.map(([name, result]) => (
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row">{sourceLabel(name)}</th>
                  <td>{String(result.seen ?? 0)}</td>
                  <td>{String(result.changed ?? 0)}</td>
                  <td>
                    {result.implemented === false
                      ? 'not implemented'
                      : result.reachable === false
                        ? 'unreachable'
                        : 'ok'}
                  </td>
                </tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        )}
      </>
    )}
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .sources {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    border-collapse: collapse;
    font-size: var(--font-size-small);
  }

  .sources th,
  .sources td {
    padding: var(--space-2) var(--space-3);
    border-bottom: var(--border-width-hairline) solid var(--border-subtle);
    text-align: start;
  }

  .sources thead th {
    color: var(--text-secondary);
    font-size: var(--font-size-caption);
    letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }

  ul {
    padding-inline-start: var(--space-4);
  }

  li + li {
    margin-block-start: var(--space-3);
  }

  /* The scope rule, quoted rather than paraphrased: it is somebody's sentence
     and the catalogue is answerable to it, not to our summary of it. */
  .rule {
    margin: var(--space-4) 0;
    padding-inline-start: var(--space-4);
    border-inline-start: var(--panel-bar-width) solid var(--panel-accent-bar);
    max-width: var(--font-measure-prose);
    font-size: var(--font-size-body);
  }

  /* A definition list, not a table: the terms are of very different lengths and
     a two-column table forces either a cramped column or a lot of white space.
     A square-cornered left accent bar per kind, at the panel bar width, as
     design §2.3 allows: it groups the specific types under their parent
     without filling a surface. */
  .defs {
    margin-block-start: var(--space-5);
    padding-inline-start: var(--space-4);
    border-inline-start: var(--panel-bar-width) solid var(--border-subtle);
  }

  .defs h4 {
    margin-block-end: var(--space-2);
  }

  .defs__list {
    margin-block: var(--space-3) 0;
  }

  .defs__list dt {
    margin-block-start: var(--space-3);
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-medium);
  }

  .defs__list dd {
    margin-block-start: var(--space-1);
    margin-inline-start: 0;
    max-width: var(--font-measure-prose);
    color: var(--text-secondary);
  }

  /* Underlined at rest, unlike links elsewhere on the site. The site convention
     is colour at rest and an underline on hover, which works because the link
     colour carries enough contrast against body text. Here the surrounding text
     is already `--text-secondary`, so colour alone no longer distinguishes the
     link and axe fails it (link-in-text-block). An underline is the fix WCAG
     actually asks for, rather than a colour that only just clears the ratio. */
  .defs__list dd a {
    text-decoration: underline;
  }
</style>
